          "content": [
            {
              "text": "{\"problem\": {\"theorem\": \"Define $f_{n}:[0,1] \\\\rightarrow \\\\mathbb{R}$ by the equation $f_{n}(x)=x^{n}$. Show that the sequence $\\\\left(f_{n}\\\\right)$ does not converge uniformly.\", \"assumptions\": [], \"domain\": \"topology\", \"explicit_subgoals\": []}, \"candidates\": [{\"candidate_id\": \"choice-1\", \"text\": \"Let\\n\\\\[\\nf(x)=\\\\begin{cases}0,&0\\\\le x<1,\\\\\\\\1,&x=1.\\\\end{cases}\\n\\\\]\\nFor every \\\\(x\\\\in[0,1)\\\\), \\\\(x^n\\\\to0\\\\), while \\\\(f_n(1)=1\\\\) for every \\\\(n\\\\). Thus \\\\(f_n\\\\to f\\\\) pointwise. If \\\\((f_n)\\\\) converged uniformly, its uniform limit would necessarily be this pointwise limit \\\\(f\\\\).\\n\\nTake \\\\(\\\\varepsilon=\\\\tfrac14\\\\). For every \\\\(N\\\\in\\\\mathbb Z_+\\\\), set \\\\(n=N\\\\) and \\\\(x_N=2^{-1/N}\\\\). Then \\\\(x_N<1\\\\), so \\\\(f(x_N)=0\\\\), whereas\\n\\\\[\\nf_N(x_N)=\\\\left(2^{-1/N}\\\\right)^N=\\\\frac12.\\n\\\\]\\nConsequently,\\n\\\\[\\n|f_N(x_N)-f(x_N)|=\\\\frac12>\\\\varepsilon.\\n\\\\]\\nHence no \\\\(N\\\\) satisfies the definition of uniform convergence, and therefore \\\\((f_n)\\\\) does not converge uniformly on \\\\([0,1]\\\\).\"}, {\"candidate_id\": \"choice-2\", \"text\": \"For each \\\\(x\\\\in[0,1)\\\\), we have \\\\(x^n\\\\to0\\\\), while \\\\(f_n(1)=1\\\\) for every \\\\(n\\\\). Thus the pointwise limit is\\n\\\\[\\nf(x)=\\\\begin{cases}0,&0\\\\le x<1,\\\\\\\\1,&x=1.\\\\end{cases}\\n\\\\]\\nFix \\\\(\\\\varepsilon_0=\\\\tfrac12\\\\). For every \\\\(N\\\\in\\\\mathbb Z_+\\\\), take \\\\(n=N\\\\) and \\\\(x_N=2^{-1/N}\\\\in[0,1)\\\\). Then \\\\(f(x_N)=0\\\\) and\\n\\\\[\\n|f_N(x_N)-f(x_N)|=x_N^N=\\\\tfrac12.\\n\\\\]\\nHence, for every \\\\(N\\\\), there exist \\\\(n\\\\ge N\\\\) and \\\\(x\\\\in[0,1]\\\\) for which \\\\(|f_n(x)-f(x)|\\\\ge\\\\varepsilon_0\\\\). Therefore \\\\((f_n)\\\\) does not converge uniformly on \\\\([0,1]\\\\).\"}, {\"candidate_id\": \"choice-3\", \"text\": \"For each \\\\(x\\\\in[0,1)\\\\), we have \\\\(x^n\\\\to0\\\\), while \\\\(f_n(1)=1\\\\) for every \\\\(n\\\\). Thus \\\\((f_n)\\\\) converges pointwise to\\n\\\\[\\nf(x)=\\\\begin{cases}0,&0\\\\le x<1,\\\\\\\\1,&x=1.\\\\end{cases}\\n\\\\]\\nTake \\\\(\\\\varepsilon=\\\\tfrac12\\\\). For every \\\\(N\\\\in\\\\mathbb Z_+\\\\), set \\\\(n=N\\\\) and \\\\(x=2^{-1/N}\\\\). Then \\\\(x\\\\in[0,1)\\\\), so \\\\(f(x)=0\\\\), whereas\\n\\\\[\\n|f_n(x)-f(x)|=x^N=\\\\frac12.\\n\\\\]\\nHence, for every \\\\(N\\\\), there exist \\\\(n\\\\ge N\\\\) and \\\\(x\\\\in[0,1]\\\\) such that \\\\(|f_n(x)-f(x)|\\\\ge\\\\varepsilon\\\\). Therefore \\\\((f_n)\\\\) does not converge uniformly to its pointwise limit. Since any uniform limit must also be the pointwise limit, the sequence does not converge uniformly to any function on \\\\([0,1]\\\\).\"}]}",
              "text_elements": [],
              "type": "text"
            }
